WEBVTT

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:12.530
 You can be so resourceful and humanistic when you really empower people to have voice and when you're starting from the idea that they should be empowered and dignified.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:21.000
 And so not everything is going to work, and not all Valerianci's programs work, and some of the ones to get to the poorest citizens, they still haven't cracked that nut.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:33.140
 It's very difficult. But this idea of trying to get everyone together and talk it through in structured ways, creating spaces of participation, I think allows you to be resourceful in all these other interesting ways that we can't sit here and figure out.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.040
 There's going to be some cool things that people can think about if you actually can get them together and create that.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:47.190
 My guest today on Delicious Revolution is Jahi Chappell.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:55.010
 He's a political agroecologist with training in ecology and evolutionary biology, science and technology studies, and chemical engineering.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:01.950
 He's a senior research fellow at the Center for Agroecology, Water, and Resilience at Coventry University in the UK.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:09.420
 He's a fellow at Food First, and he's an adjunct faculty member at the School of the Environment in Washington State University.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:17.190
 Jahi has recently published a book called Beginning to End Hunger, Food and the Environment in Belo Horizonte, Brazil, and beyond.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:25.170
 It's rooted in his field of research in Belo Horizonte over more than a decade, and it presents a far-reaching analysis of how to end hunger,

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:31.500
 what's keeping us as a society from doing it, and how we might overcome the many obstacles in our way.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:40.040
 I spoke to Jahi in the cafeteria of the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization in Rome during a vibrant symposium on agroecology.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:52.030
 Jahi has written a new book on hunger and the experience of greatly reducing hunger in Belo Horizonte called Beginning to End Hunger.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.530
 It's one of the best books I've ever read on the subject.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.010
 Jahi is a friend, and I'm really excited to talk to you about this.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:02.970
 Yeah, thanks.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.020
 Yeah, I'm excited to have a chat about it.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:10.120
 This is, I guess, my second talk about the book, and as you might know from your own experiences,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.850
 just the way academic research works.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:18.420
 There's such a gulf often between when you do the work and talking about it and analyzing it and the book coming out.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.400
 So, yeah, I'm glad I'll be talking to you for one of the first ones.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:28.600
 Can we start just with the story of Belo Horizonte, and when did you start doing research there,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.240
 and when did this process of heavily investing and actually ending hunger begin there?

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:42.900
 So I started studying there actually in 2003, which is 10 years to the year after the programs were founded in 1993.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.890
 I sort of came across it by chance.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:52.350
 I was trying to think of places I could study connections between food and biodiversity and conservation,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.330
 and just from the start of my graduate career,

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.170
 it was crazy to me that we're worried about this conflict between agriculture and food security

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.890
 when we produce enough food, and so we keep talking about how to scale up, how to intensify,

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.750
 how to produce more for this growing population.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:15.020
 And so Belo Horizonte was this great example where they knew they had enough food,

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.330
 and Brazil in general has more than enough food for its population,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:25.030
 and so they really realized that what they need to address is food access and the right to food.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:31.060
 So the program started in 1993 with the mayor, Patronus Ananias de Silva,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.860
 who was a member of the Workers' Party, and the right to food was really a core value for him,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.140
 both as a member of the Workers' Party, a leftist party in Brazil, and as a Catholic.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.340
 And the thing that I guess that the overall context, besides the fact of the right to food,

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:54.420
 was that the Workers' Party had been spending decades, arguably, working with social movements,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.830
 working with a lot of other progressive thinkers, including one of the people who sort of founded

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:06.270
 food security analysis based on poverty and power, Jose de Castro, who wrote a book in the 20s

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.250
 that really influenced these programs much later.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.940
 So that all came together, movement building, conversations, and creating a shared agenda

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.360
 in 88 between the Workers' Party and movements that, to my mind, is what set the fact that they were

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.620
 able to get this in Belo Horizonte in 93, that they'd worked through a lot of these conversations.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.380
 And when the PT started coming to power, they had a mandate, they had people behind them,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.670
 and they had a clear set of approaches they wanted to take that was holistic and firmly rights-based.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.810
 What was that political opening that let them start to actually implement some of those programs?

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.250
 And then what were some of the concrete programs that they were doing?

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:49.280
 So it actually was literally called the Abertura, the opening up in Brazil, that happened in the 80s.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.080
 They had a dictatorship for about 20 years, and social rights had started to open up.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.500
 People were starting to protest.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.240
 Arguably, it was one of the first times that movements really were forming in Brazil,

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.900
 including this is the same time that the Landless Rural Workers Movement was starting to come together.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:14.140
 So the flood of people really concerned about democracy and about the dictatorship and about social rights.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.380
 Brazil is still one of the most unequal countries in the world, and it was much worse several decades ago.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.720
 So when the dictatorship started pulling back, people got very organized and came together across different issues as well.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:34.900
 Poverty, housing, homelessness, the right to food, the right to be free from oppression, social demonstration.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:44.950
 And the first democratically elected president after the dictatorship, Fernando Collor, ended up being fairly corrupt,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.970
 as well as being very neoliberal and scaling back some of the policies that were helping people.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:57.930
 Because even the dictatorship had had some policies that were meant to, as part of a modern state, help people in social welfare terms.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.870
 So Collor was eventually impeached after hundreds of thousands of people protested against his government.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:11.290
 So this opening up, this gathering together of many different social forces for change and for social rights,

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.270
 really is what prefigured the ability of Beto Rezanchi to have their programs.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:21.850
 So in 88, there was a parallel Congress that the PT had formed, parallel to the actually elected government,

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.310
 sort of a shadow Congress like we have in Parliament in the United Kingdom, where I live now.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:31.200
 And so they had made this agenda that had, I don't know how many points, but like I say,

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.380
 like food security, political rights, voting rights, consultation with civil society.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.020
 And so the PT slowly built its way to power.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:45.260
 By 93, they had elected a large number of mayors and governors.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:52.090
 And because all of the countries, I think 400 and some municipalities, vote for mayor on the same day,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.350
 you can really see if there's a swing.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:00.090
 So the PT suddenly had gone from, I don't know the exact numbers, but like let's say 5 to 30 percent of the mayorships.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:04.010
 So they had clear mandates and several other progressive parties.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:10.400
 So this is the same time that participatory budgeting started getting very established in Brazil.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:12.820
 And Patrus Ananias came into Belo Horizonte.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:20.360
 And my analysis is that different PT figures sort of had their own issue that they wanted to take on within this large agenda they co-created.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.240
 And so Patrus took on food and said we need to do it holistically.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.880
 So I should answer your question about some of the programs.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:38.850
 And so a big part of my analysis is the fact that they really said you have to attack hunger from multiple points of view in order to make progress.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:45.060
 And so one of those was starting with youth because hunger in your youth can affect you your whole life.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:51.170
 And so Brazil actually has had school meal programs for many decades, but they weren't necessarily very good.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:58.590
 So Belo Horizonte's first issue was they said to the mayor, got together several departments to actually again sort of form a joint agenda.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:03.030
 And he said to the education department, you receive the federal funding for school meals.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.120
 You should be thinking about pedagogy and those elements of teaching our children.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:14.470
 So we'd like to take that budget from you, which no one likes, but that way you won't have to administer food, which is not your area.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:20.220
 And we'll put it in this new office where we'll make sure that they're fresh, nutritious, healthy, balanced.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.640
 We'll make sure that children who don't have enough food at home get access to it.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:24.800
 So they did that.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:32.730
 They had these fresh cooked school meals that to me, one of the really emblematic and beautiful parts of the school meals and getting kids to eat more healthily.

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:38.970
 When I was there one time, someone with me in the study group said, well, kids don't like vegetables.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.170
 You know, how do you get them to eat vegetables?

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.740
 Because that's, you know, they have to eat vegetables supposedly now.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.440
 And they said, well, we have like five or six varieties because Brazil has a lot of diversity in food.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.800
 And, you know, the kid tries one one day and one the other day.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:57.670
 And because they have that choice, we found that after about a month, food waste went way down because they just they found the one that they wanted.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.230
 And they're able to choose that.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.870
 And that really got them into it.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.730
 So they're like, oh, I like, you know, this, I don't like Covey, I like that.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.460
 So I just think that's emblematic of their approach.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.150
 They took really human centered and humanistic and empowering.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:17.850
 So there's about 27 programs they ended up creating.

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:29.510
 Some other important ones around access are the ABCs, the Abastasers or Saka Loins means big bag, like the bag you use for carrying produce home.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:41.730
 And so they had a series of these big bag produce shops that local partners who are private business owners would agree to sell 40 products or some set of 40 products at a single price.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.530
 Mostly fruits and vegetables, but also, I think, some personal hygiene items.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:54.890
 And if they agree to sell those at this lower than market average price, but still with researchers calculated to be above the cost of productions, they shouldn't be losing money.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:10:07.890
 If they sold these 40 products all for the same price, the city often had another piece of property that they would let them lease very cheaply somewhere else that maybe they could, you know, a richer neighborhood where they could sell produce at higher prices and be happy.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:08.990
 I find it interesting.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.070
 I think this was their version of private-public partnerships.

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:16.860
 But because of the particular power in this time period of the government, you know, it was very much in the government's terms.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:26.190
 So one of the nice things about this program, about the ABCs or Saka Loins, was because you had many different produce, fruits and vegetables, for the same price per kilo.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:32.640
 At some point, it was like 89 centavos per kilo, about 40 cents per kilo.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:37.940
 The same price for all the different produce, so you could mix and match, so it really encouraged dietary diversity.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.400
 Again, in this empowering way, not like saying you have to, you know, eat this and get this.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.550
 It was just to give people lots of choice in an easily accessible fashion.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:53.980
 And these are open all over the city, but often near major byways, so people could walk to and from work or the bus stop or whatnot.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:11:02.830
 So I should maybe just quickly talk about the third flagship program that people often mention, which is the People's Restaurants or the Popular Restaurants.

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.700
 And they now have four of them.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.660
 And these are cafeterias.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.980
 That's a very common Brazilian lunch institution is you go someplace and you buy food by kilo.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.460
 And so this is similar to that.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.160
 You go to the cafeteria and there's fresh-cooked food.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:24.430
 They invented these new large-scale pots so they could cook beans and rice in these really huge batches.

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.330
 But it's fresh-cooked, you know, while you're there.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:29.870
 And people stand in line for this 50-cent meal, basically.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:37.880
 That's a healthy, balanced meal designed by local chefs trying to take into account local culture as well as tastiness.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:43.090
 And really important to them based on food with dignity.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:49.210
 So not only did everyone pay in the original model so that no matter what your income was, you deserve this.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:50.230
 You paid for it.

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:55.250
 And if you couldn't pay for it, they would try and find a way to get you in a line and maybe give you 50 cents.

00:11:55.710 --> 00:12:03.830
 This food with dignity idea also was reflected in that all of the meals were on these metal trays with real silverware.

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:05.770
 But it was real silverware.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:11.370
 And this big thing about when some of the Brazilians from that program saw a food bank in Canada.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:13.970
 And she said, well, why do they have styrofoam cups?

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:14.630
 What is this?

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.450
 And someone asked her, well, but don't people steal silverware?

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.330
 She said, well, yes, we did find they steal silverware.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.750
 But at some point, people have enough silverware.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:29.650
 And for us, that was a price worth paying that eventually it tapered off.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.570
 And we want people to have real silverware because they deserve it.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:34.390
 It's dignified.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.070
 It's part of their right to food.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:41.510
 And so that's been a really powerful model that you have 12,000 people a day to the four restaurants.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:47.770
 These strike me as very humanistic interventions, but they're also very resourceful, right?

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:50.630
 About using what's available to meet those needs.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:56.610
 Yeah, I mean, I think, and maybe, I'm not sure if I brought this out in the book to the extent that I wanted.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:12.300
 But as you will know from having read it, just in the end, one of my big conclusions is that enabling democracy and voice in new ways, not just democracy, going to the voting booth, but participation has been also a really key part of it.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:26.080
 They have a food policy council that has been more and less effective at different times, but has been a really key to the founding and has spread throughout Brazil, these food policy councils that don't make laws, but they have a lot of influence.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:34.230
 But I think what's exciting is that you have these things like food policy councils and these other consultative bodies that are sort of democratic innovations.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:44.490
 We're at right now a meeting that talks a lot about innovation, and a lot of people from social movements have been emphasizing that that can be changing social conditions, can be an innovation.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:54.780
 And so I feel like you can be so resourceful and humanistic when you really empower people to have voice and when you're starting from the idea that they should be empowered and dignified.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:03.250
 And so not everything is going to work, and not all of the Zanchi's programs work, and some of the ones to get to the poorest citizens, they still haven't cracked that nut.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:04.030
 It's very difficult.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:19.980
 But this idea of trying to get everyone together and talk it through in structured ways, so it's not just publicized, it's not anarchic, but creating spaces of participation, I think allows you to be resourceful in all these other interesting ways that we can't sit here and figure out.

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:24.880
 There's going to be some cool things that people can think about if you actually can get them together and create that.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.320
 What was the impact of these programs over time?

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:36.690
 From the numbers that we have, it seems like infant mortality went down by about 75% between 1993 and 2002.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:43.330
 It's hard to know how much of that was SMASAN, which is the acronym or the name for these programs in Belo Horizonte.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:48.630
 Infant malnutrition, at least in one study, went down similarly by about 50% to 60%.

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:56.630
 Something that I saw very interesting, I've worked in Belo Horizonte, like I said, since 2003, but when I was writing the book, I was relooking up some numbers.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.850
 Diabetes, hospitalization due to diabetes went down by, I think, 20% or 30%.

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:08.300
 And again, it's hard to draw straight lines because they didn't have sort of a test group, a control group, and not for Belo Horizonte.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:19.990
 But they did, in the national censuses, go from being eighth or ninth in consumption of fresh fruits and vegetables in 92 to being first and second for many years.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:23.590
 And they've fluctuated in the top five now, but first and second for quite a while.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:32.070
 And so it's hard not to think that that's connected to the decrease in infant malnutrition, infant mortality, and definitely diabetes, these possible diet changes.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:43.360
 And then the national government starts to notice what's happening there, and there's a process of taking these ideas and implementing them on a greater scale.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:44.700
 How did that work?

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.500
 And did it work as well as it did in Belo Horizonte?

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:58.510
 I would say it was revolutionary what the national government did, but it didn't necessarily take on the full revolutionary suite of things from Belo Horizonte.

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:09.150
 So I should say that in 2004, then President Lula started Fomizaro, Zero Hunger, and they learned a lot from Belo Horizonte's example.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:15.110
 But it also was based, again, on a lot of the strategizing they'd been doing for, well, at that point, for about five years.

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:20.950
 Since 88, they had formally come together with movements, but, of course, going beyond that, organizing against the dictatorship.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:27.520
 So Fomizaro really was born out of this contract against hunger that they'd been working on since then.

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:32.910
 And Patrus Ananias and Belo Horizonte had taken part of it and tried it at a city scale.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:41.600
 Now at a national scale, they had first focused on income redistribution, which Belo Horizonte didn't do, but that was part of their sensibility.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:47.920
 Again, like the popular restaurants, supposedly, I think this didn't always happen, but they were meant to have a social worker there.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:57.040
 So if someone did have actually zero money, not only did they give them some money to get in line and pay, but also connect them with someone to try and get them connected with jobs training.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:12.110
 So this redistribution and sort of trying to build people up in their own capacity and livelihood, I think, is the core element that Bolsa Familia, which was part of Fomizaro, which is this redistribution, came about.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:30.960
 The technical term for it is conditional cash transfer, but it's the idea that if you have these minimum elements of behavior, which has some maybe anti-liberatory implications, but if you keep your children in school and immunized, which are not in themselves bad things at all, then you get, and you're below a certain poverty line, then you get a certain amount of money from the government every year.

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:56.980
 And Brazil was the first one to really scale that idea up to this level, and they saw what Angus Wright, a historian who's worked in Latin America extensively, said between that and the minimum wage laws and the attempts to guarantee employment or create employment, that probably Brazil, between 93 and 2010 or so, saw probably the greatest reduction in poverty severity in modern times.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:05.650
 And so, which is very laudable at the same time, and they usually acknowledge this to the national government.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:09.810
 They didn't really go beyond, too much beyond Fomizaro, certainly not in scale.

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:16.910
 There were many other programs, or rather Bolsa Familia, many other programs in Fomizaro, including supporting smallholder farmers.

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:28.390
 And this was actually quite revolutionary, even though it was still way too small, that they had a department, basically, of family farms that was separate from the Department of Agribusiness.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:37.970
 And that department, I think, got up to having funding that was maybe 8% to 10% of the size of the budget for the agribusiness, which, again, is not necessarily as much as we'd like.

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:40.450
 That's pretty significant.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:42.370
 You have to start somewhere.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:56.120
 And even though right now we've seen a closing of some spaces, we were just listening to various people talk, including a gentleman, Paulo Pedersen, from Brazil, working in agroecology and family farming.

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:16.070
 And the reason that he and his network, the Association of Family Farming and Agroecology, the groups like that have become nationally prominent is because even though there wasn't enough support from my point of view, the support from the government for small farming and agroecology before the current administration, I think it was enough to catalyze it.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:21.030
 It was enough to take it to a new level, even though now it's fighting for its life in many ways.

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:27.210
 So there were these other elements besides the income redistribution.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:31.530
 But like I said, I think the overall structure didn't really change.

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:35.370
 And a key problem is that Brazil became a lot less unequal.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:43.510
 But they were basically funding FOMIZERO out of economic growth and not out of taxes.

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:48.620
 The rich don't pay significant taxes compared to their wealth in Brazil.

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:54.300
 And that's part of the problem now is that if they wanted to keep funding their programs, they would have to tax the rich.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:20:00.710
 And that's something that's still hard, very, very hard to do in Brazil and part of the elements that are going on right now.

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:11.470
 So those are the elements that I think didn't quite live up to Barazanchi's overall goal of food with dignity and really using the power of the government to create more space for rights.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:27.650
 And just one emblematic example maybe to hold all together was for many years, Smasan and Barazanchi refused to fund food banks because their idea was that we shouldn't be redistributing food people don't want.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.130
 We should be making sure they get access to food of their own.

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:41.820
 They eventually did decide that there were so many Catholic charities and different groups that were donating food that it would help to have someplace where they could make sure it's safe and process correctly and get to people who need it.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:45.010
 But they were very resistant to the food bank model entirely.

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:51.750
 And nationally, you didn't see that same resistance, that there was some funding for food banks at the national level as part of FOMIZERO.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:57.440
 And so they didn't think they could go maybe to that full liberatory model even to start with.

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:02.250
 And so with so many things, there's pluses and minuses, you can see their point of view to an extent.

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:07.270
 But I think philosophically, you can see that distance a little bit from Barazanchi.

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:20.040
 We'll circle back to talking about what's happening in Brazil now, but we should probably contextualize that we're sitting here at the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization up on the top floor, kind of looking out over Rome.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:25.960
 And there's a pretty straight line from that experience of Brazil's change in agricultural and food policy.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:29.540
 And the director general of the FAO is from Brazil.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:37.840
 And now there's this opening of a space to even talk about this kind of systemic change in the FAO.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:40.680
 It's a small space in the FAO, but there's an opening of something.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.660
 So I see that it's kind of this as part of that trajectory.

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:46.330
 Yeah, yeah, no, I think that's absolutely right.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:55.550
 I remember something that my mom, who's a retired professor of social work, had said when I was talking about the growth of the food movement in the context of the United States.

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:57.430
 But I was saying, I'm afraid it's going to be a fad.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:01.920
 And she said, well, anything that starts out with a small group of people will seem like a fad.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:03.490
 The problem isn't the fad.

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:07.630
 The problem is you want it to go beyond that small group of people and small amount of time.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:11.130
 But don't fight the fad part, fight to make sure it's durable.

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:23.170
 And so I think that's what we see right now, FAO, with agroecology and social change, that even people within the system, within the FAO, are saying, yes, of course, we need transformation away from industrial agriculture.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.970
 And, of course, hunger isn't about a lack of food production.

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:28.090
 And so it's sort of the flavor of the month.

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:37.460
 And the job of us and the job of activists and allied academic thinkers and movements is to keep that pressure because things come and go.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:40.070
 And this is exactly what we saw in Brazil.

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:45.760
 I mentioned Jorge Castro published a book called Geography of Hunger in the 20s in Brazil.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:57.760
 And then dictator Getulio Vargas decided to put into practice these ideas about basically food access and making sure people had jobs and therefore economic well-being and ability to buy food.

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:04.220
 And created a secretary to a department of social services to administer these ideas.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:10.570
 And then Getulio Vargas went in and out of office, was elected, was a dictator again, was thrown out.

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:16.610
 But his successors ended up not continuing these social programs and slowly withering and killing them.

00:23:16.750 --> 00:23:19.310
 And Jorge Castro actually came to the FAO.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:24.350
 He was a representative for Brazil and ended up staying in Rome because of the political changes.

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:28.730
 You know, he got to see his life's work go in many different directions.

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:33.350
 I believe he died before Belo Horizonte and certainly before Formizero.

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:44.800
 But without him, we wouldn't have had what we had and had that dramatic decrease that we saw in Brazil at large and within Belo Horizonte, which has two and a half million people.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:45.620
 I haven't mentioned that.

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:50.810
 This is not a small city or just, oh, they're able to do it because it's just like a block large.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:52.970
 It's a very large city.

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:59.020
 And actually within Brazil right now, it seems like the secretariat is still doing all right.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:10.700
 I haven't heard anything very recently, but there's an initial worry because the new mayor, who's the fifth since Patros Ananias, which is already a lot of longevity in political terms.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.710
 The new mayor wasn't certain how he would treat the department.

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:16.530
 They've gone back and forth with different leadership.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:23.730
 But he came in and he did fire all the staff of the city, which has happened sometimes in some countries.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:25.170
 And that's not abnormal.

00:24:25.930 --> 00:24:26.830
 Thousands of people.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.310
 And then he rehired the ones he wanted.

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:30.430
 And so who knew what's going to happen?

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:33.670
 But he rehired some of the key people from Smasan who have been there since the beginning.

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:39.530
 And they tell me that the secretary, the person in charge of the department, is actually one of the best people they've had.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:43.040
 And so you see different things at different levels and they come and go.

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:51.790
 But I think that underlying struggle and when you have people involved and have the citizen space, that's, I think, what leads you to success in the long term.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:25:12.090
 One of the really important pieces of analysis in your book has to do with the fact that they were able to achieve the gains that they did in Belo Horizonte and in Brazil disarm that argument that, oh, we can't do anything about hunger or that it's too hard to address the massive amount of hunger that happens in the world.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:21.570
 And if we've seen it done and done for over a long time and there's clear lessons to learn from it, why are we not doing that?

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:23.990
 Like, it begs the ethical question.

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.590
 And you have a lot of analysis about why we're not doing that.

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:28.210
 Mm-hmm.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:33.240
 Yeah, I mean, I think there's obviously there's a lot of different elements of it.

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:39.260
 But to me, one of the core ones, I think almost all of us, almost every single person wants people to be well off.

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:43.070
 And the problem often is, well, I don't know how to do it.

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:43.950
 How do I affect it?

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:45.530
 What am I supposed to do about it?

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:53.010
 And in Belo and in Brazil at large, one of the things to remember, like I said, is it took these decades.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:54.270
 It started in the 20s.

00:25:54.830 --> 00:26:01.900
 And I used to like to say to my students just in modern era, when we had the civil rights era in the United States, they didn't know they were going to win.

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:03.960
 Like, now it looks like fate.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:08.940
 But they weren't going like, oh, yeah, a couple of years from now, we're going to have, you know, half of our agenda.

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:11.800
 And then decades later to get other parts of it.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.960
 But, you know, they didn't know that they were going to get the U.S. Civil Rights Act.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:16.980
 They, you know, were pushing for various things.

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.530
 So, you know, in the middle of it, it still can feel very hopeless.

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:30.670
 And so I think it's holding on to that hope or what Eber de Souza, who was another activist, very influential in how Beresonche came about.

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:43.750
 He led a movement that helped lead to the impeachment of Kolor in the 90s and then formed a movement called the Citizens Action Movement Against Hunger and For Life, which doesn't even have a good acronym.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:47.330
 It's quite I'm not sure if they couldn't have said that every time they introduced themselves.

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:53.400
 But anyway, but Eber de Souza, in an interview, people are saying, well, you know, how do you keep at this?

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:55.420
 You know, do you have hope for the future?

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:58.090
 And he said, well, I'm not some stupid optimist.

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:01.820
 I'm an active optimist, which I just that that's in the book.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.240
 I just I think that epitomizes so much of what has to happen.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.720
 We can't just sit back and hope things will get better.

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:11.290
 It is going to take work and we don't know when that work will pay off.

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:25.340
 And that, to me, is one of the enduring messages of the book that even right now, with things very bad in a number of countries from various points of view in terms of our democracies, that it has to be the long game.

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:52.020
 But the thing to me that that's key, if I have a short ish message, I'm not good at short, but a short ish message is that the simplistic explanation of how things happen in Belo Horizonte is that you had movement sensibility and agenda come together with activist academics and intellectuals coming together with people eventually who are in elected office.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:28:01.680
 And when you have the policy analysts and academic experts on the same page with the same agenda as the movement, you can pressure policymakers.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:05.150
 And when you have sympathetic policymakers, get things actually done.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:13.370
 And this is based on something called the multiple streams analysis and political science, which I won't get into the details of, but that's that's the core of it to me.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:29.460
 And this building continuity and trust between these different sectors, between people who have resources, both intellectual and financial in terms of and power wise, in terms of thinkers and academics and people in the FAO.

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:41.760
 When you really make common cause, and from my point of view, to a certain amount of serving the movement, being accountable to movements and to groups that come from the ground, you're not going to agree with everything.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:49.120
 But accountability means finding what you can agree on and pushing for that and building that shared agenda.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:53.520
 And when you do that, I think that's when things are likely to happen.

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:54.460
 It still takes some time.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:56.460
 You have to wait for the right window, the window opportunity.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:29:04.400
 But the way to make that window come about and the way to make it effective to me is to already have those links and have worked out your common agenda.

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:06.950
 And that's the part that I think is very challenging.

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:16.130
 But in a way, to me, it's from when I was researching this, it was hopeful because the individual, it's more than just, OK, I have to try and get all my friends to vote for the right person.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:18.920
 But it's it can be fun.

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:28.460
 It's getting together with people who care about some social issue you do, which everyone has something, you know, it's urban gardening or access to quality schools or land reform.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:31.230
 There's people who are doing something you care about.

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:44.240
 And to me, it's about joining those groups, especially if you're someone who has some power as an academic or something, joining those groups, really learning and serving with and alongside and then creating this common agenda.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:47.400
 And then if you're which is, I think, a big step for a lot of us.

00:29:47.400 --> 00:30:02.540
 But if you're really ambitious, you've already done that, then connecting with other people who are also struggling and a different issue and creating a shared agenda and know that you're not all going to go at the same time, but be ready to say, OK, well, we've got our common people's agenda around education, food, land reform.

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:07.540
 And so when the right politician comes in, the sympathetic ear comes in like we're going to pounce together.

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:15.100
 And I do want to say Reverend William Barber and the momentum and forward together is a really exciting movement in the United States right now.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:21.460
 And I think that's sort of exactly the analysis that they have, that we have to look at social justice across all these dimensions.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:29.970
 And we can do that and realize that it's not going to be quickly, but we can build leaders from the ground up over time.

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:33.240
 That you can really, you can get the change that you want.

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:36.360
 And it might go back again, but we can get it in the long term.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:43.860
 And that organizing can happen during a time of authoritarianism like we have in the United States now or Brazil now too.

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:46.400
 That organizing can be very productive.

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:47.140
 Absolutely.

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:51.910
 And I would say a really, really important part is that is the time to organize.

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:53.430
 And it's a time when people get activated.

00:30:53.570 --> 00:31:02.020
 But I think it's so key not to get organized to get out the guy or woman you hate or think it's causing damage.

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:04.960
 It's organizing to get this shared analysis and agenda.

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:18.140
 Because when that person eventually does fall or kicked out or pushed out, corruption, whatever happens, just normal elections, we like to think, okay, thank goodness we can get back to normal, which normal is not that great anyway for many people.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:23.620
 But if we don't have a shared agenda, then we'll just go back to these, the fights that we see right now, but just get larger.

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:28.850
 When you have power and the ability to get your agenda done, it gets worse if you don't have a shared agenda.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:37.460
 And so the organizing and figuring out the compromises within ourselves, not compromising our values, but realizing that we're not going to get everything at once.

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:42.680
 So if we can get on the same page for a variety of issues, we can push the ones that are opportune.

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:44.300
 And this is, again, the long term.

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:48.700
 We can all have our turn, but we have to figure out that strategicness.

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:57.700
 And I would just say one of the things that's been fascinating me these past couple of years is just looking back at civil rights in the United States again, just even a little bit.

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.930
 And we forget, I mean, there was deep strategy there.

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:04.860
 And some of it is kind of mercenary.

00:32:05.260 --> 00:32:10.660
 And I don't know how happy that makes me, but I think we have to at least think in these kinds of terms.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:16.380
 With Rosa Parks, for example, she was the third or fourth person who refused to give up her seat and was arrested.

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:19.360
 There's a woman, I think her name was Blythe Colvin.

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:23.700
 I might be wrong, but there are two or three women who had been arrested before for this.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:25.300
 But one of them was an unwed mother.

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:29.390
 And they said, well, this is not going to get us where we need to go.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:34.570
 Rosa Parks was this, you know, well-spoken, had her lines thought out, church lady.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.500
 And so she was the one they said, well, okay, now we're going to move.

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:42.030
 And they kept telling everyone, you know, hold your powder, hold your powder.

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:44.570
 And that's really higher than the stay of Twitter and all that.

00:32:44.710 --> 00:32:51.650
 And I feel like that is the legitimate side of when people complain about people crusading against various things or wanting purity.

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:56.310
 I'm not, I don't think, I think we should be happy that more people are speaking out against things.

00:32:56.710 --> 00:33:04.190
 But it is true, I think, that we need to, if you really want it to happen, more important than making sure your other friend is completely pure on the right terms or whatever,

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:13.510
 is to make sure that you have your shit together and figuring out internally what you're going to push for and when.

00:33:13.930 --> 00:33:24.660
 So that when you do get the juncture, you can get a variety of stuff done, but not just picking at each other in these, these, they're not small battles, but the organizing anyway.

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:29.740
 So that's the way to say it is organizing is very different than opinionating and you need both.

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:32.420
 But the organizing is the part that I think we're really lacking.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:41.170
 And that's just working together that seems to be often out of fad in the United States, but so necessary and so powerful when it does happen.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:46.250
 This is a bit of a hard question because it's only happening now.

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:54.380
 But do you see that those multiple streams strengthening right now on this, at this conference on agroecology at FAO?

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:55.840
 Is that what's happening here?

00:33:58.940 --> 00:34:00.540
 That's, that's, that's Trixie.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:13.900
 You know, I hadn't thought of it in those terms right now, but actually I do, I think we do, because I think, I mean, we have a lot of people here from sort of the academic analysis background,

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:21.140
 as well as a lot of people from, from the base themselves, from pastoralists, fisher folk, farmers, of course, small scale farmers.

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:25.430
 And I feel like from what I've seen, we're pretty united.

00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:34.820
 There's a shared analysis of a need for system change, of a need to involve, have rights and women's rights particularly, and equality and representation.

00:34:35.170 --> 00:34:50.070
 There's a, really, a shared sensibility, I think, which is the, the policy stream, the, the thinker policy make designers coming together with the movements, certain policy thinkers and analysts.

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:55.170
 And I think the other thing that we see is, of course, the internal tensions in FAO.

00:34:55.450 --> 00:34:58.700
 And for a variety of reasons, there's only so radical they can be.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:05.360
 And then there's also just how radical, you know, they want to be the individuals and institution in terms of what, what seems like they can do.

00:35:05.780 --> 00:35:16.570
 And I think one of the things that was interesting thinking about Belo Horizonte in Brazil is there's often this tension between inside or outside, between critique and collaboration or co-optation.

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:21.640
 And to me, there's just a partial resolution that we can think of in terms of we need both.

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:30.070
 But to me, the people who are inside are empowered, this can sound counterintuitive, but by opposition from the outside.

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:34.740
 And I think even people inside don't appreciate that in my, usually in my point of view.

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:49.090
 But there's a famous story that Obama used to say that supposedly didn't really happen this way, but that when Lyndon Johnson was trying to advocate and get the U.S. civil rights bill passed, that Martin Luther King and others said, like, you have to, you have to make this happen.

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:51.060
 And he said, well, make me.

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:55.550
 Like, get you, literally, get people out there to make me do it.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:57.070
 I can't do it unless you make me.

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:00.050
 Supposedly it didn't actually really happen or didn't happen quite like that.

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:02.080
 But I think the underlying story is there.

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:09.710
 And I actually think this is maybe getting a little far afield, but I think this is, it's very telling that Obama used to tell that story quite a lot.

00:36:10.390 --> 00:36:11.610
 It wasn't reported that much.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:15.110
 But if you look, people, certain journalists said, oh, hey, he told that story all the time.

00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:24.980
 And I think even though he complained in public when people were too far left and expecting too much, that I think there was a strategic vision there that like, yeah, you have to push me farther than I want to go.

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:27.140
 And I'm not going to, I'm not going to go along easy.

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:28.780
 I'm not going to say, yes, that's awesome.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:36.420
 So I think this opposition of politics that it's even better if we can talk to people on the inside and realize that, yeah, we're going to tell you you're not doing enough.

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:39.120
 And it's not because we don't like you or think you have the wrong values.

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:42.260
 It's because that's how you can have more space to do what's right.

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:47.860
 And I think that's really hard to personally realize.

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:51.180
 I mean, especially when you are trying to do your best near an institution.

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:55.370
 And it's not, you know, it doesn't always work that way.

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:56.830
 So it takes some planning and strategy.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:37:00.620
 But I think that's a key dynamic that we, that is, is coming together of streams.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:02.280
 And so I see that potential here.

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:07.860
 We have people inside the FAO that really believe in the whole vision, the liberatory vision of agroecology.

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:11.490
 And I think they're trying the best they can.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:24.000
 And I hope that we have or can get to an understanding where, yeah, we're demanding more, not because we don't think that you're doing as much as you can, but us demanding more right now is what's going to allow you the space to do more.

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:41.940
 Another big analysis that you have in your book is one that I hear being articulated here, although it's not yet put into practice in the vast majority of the FAO's operation is that hunger is not about a lack of production.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:47.750
 And it's not even about a lack of distribution or just access, really.

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:55.080
 And so the way to address hunger, if you want to succeed, take a multiple approach and a movement-based approach.

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:58.330
 So are you hearing that articulated here?

00:37:58.490 --> 00:38:01.690
 Do you think that we're in a moment of paradigm change about that?

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:04.480
 Can we be hopeful that the practice will start to follow that?

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:07.230
 Wow.

00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:09.450
 That's another hard one.

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:15.500
 I do think that we are seeing a paradigm shift.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:21.240
 And the funny thing, I think, about almost all of us humans is that we don't necessarily see ourselves shifting.

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:28.820
 So I think I'm not sure people here would say that they've shifted or that they've always known or believed that hunger is about more than production or even access.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:52.310
 But I think the consistency with which pretty much all the speakers have emphasized that in varying ways that we need more democratic accountability, more participation, more voice and empowerment, real empowerment, where people are given or take the power to actually determine how things are spent, what the focuses are.

00:38:52.550 --> 00:39:07.690
 Not the sort of, I don't know, halfway empowerment where you say, I've got this budget to this kind of project with you and you can help me decide exactly what flavor of, you know, whatever, what color of car that we're going to use to drive you to prosperity, to mango metaphors.

00:39:08.790 --> 00:39:14.660
 So I do think that people are emphasizing those elements from the speaker's side.

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:19.080
 And the FAO did have a big hand in selecting who was going to speak.

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:36.920
 So even though I feel like that voice of agency, which is one of the pieces of analyzing food security from my point of view, that we often talk about availability, how many calories do we have or grains or fruits or vegetables, and accessibility, which is economic and physical access.

00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:40.980
 You know, do you have the infrastructure and do you have the money or rights to get food?

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:49.010
 Agency, the ability to change the agency, the ability to change the political system so that your rights are guaranteed and that you can collaboratively decide what rights mean.

00:39:49.790 --> 00:40:00.270
 And like we were talking about earlier, these resourceful approaches to food in Belo Horizonte, that to me is the key and really is another way of saying food sovereignty, a term that, you know, a lot of us also use.

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:10.650
 And I do feel like there's been a paradigm change already in that everyone from the FAO has so far said it's not about production.

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:18.190
 But as we were saying earlier, you know, it's a lot harder to put a lot of things into practice than to say them, of course.

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:26.850
 And so I do think we're at this juncture where we have to keep expanding our circle of allies when we can get on the same page.

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:35.610
 And that's the challenge of not being co-opted is, I think, finding the common values and then expanding your circle through ongoing dialogue.

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:39.550
 It's not just that you have common values because we all think the same thing.

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:48.160
 And that's part of the genius of Via Campesina, of the international small farmer peasants movement, is that they've had dialogues for literally decades now.

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:57.930
 And that's why they have a united voice that still also tries to allow room for diversity in context because they're fundamental values they've gotten on the same page of.

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:13.310
 And I hate sort of marketing salesmanship, but using the same words when you're talking about the same thing helps because people who are not food experts, if they hear five different terms, it's going to be hard to grasp onto what are we lobbying about.

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:19.390
 So that's, to me, the power of agroecology and food sovereignty, is people are really using those terms, it's being taken up.

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:27.280
 I think I even heard one person from a room-based agency say food sovereignty, which is a major thing.

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:40.850
 And so I think, yeah, we are at a moment of paradigm change, but that just means that we have to not only keep doing what we're doing, but keep doing it smarter and with more people and in real collaborative spaces.

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:44.470
 Wonderful, Jay. I think that's the place we should leave it.

00:41:44.470 --> 00:41:47.750
